        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Cimmerians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Caspian and Ciscaucasian steppes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch-strait">Cimmerian Bosporus</a> (Kerch Strait), in present-day Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; later māt Gamir around Gori in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, then Cappadocia and Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, with a capital at Ḫarzallē, and an eastern group near Mannai and Media</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">part of the Chernogorovka-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a> complex (9th to mid-7th centuries BC); in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a> from c. 750 BC; first recorded in 714 BC; division into western and eastern groups under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> (681–669 BC); the height of power under Dugdammî, called '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-universe">King of the Universe</a>' by 657 BC; final defeat around c. 635 BC</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">mounted archery with Scythian-type socketed arrowheads and sigmoid bows; the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusa-i">Rusa I</a> of Urartu; the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a> and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>; the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> in 644 BC and the death of Gyges; the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Artemision</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>; Animal Style art; bringing trousers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">no written records of their own; Neo-Assyrian records of the 8th to 7th centuries BC (intelligence reports, oracle texts, royal inscriptions); Graeco-Roman authors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> as Gōmer; kurgans, weapons and horse gear from the steppe, Caucasia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>; genetic studies of 2018 and 2019</span></div>
        </div>
