    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 720s BC</span><span class="rf-text">The Cimmerians help Colchis and nearby regions defeat attacks by Urartu</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">714 BC</span><span class="rf-text">The Cimmerians heavily defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusa-i">Rusa I</a> of Urartu, in the first recorded mention of them</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">679 BC</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> kills the Cimmerian king Teušpâ in a campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 675 BC</span><span class="rf-text">Under Dugdammî the western Cimmerians destroy the empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> and sack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 665–660 BC</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia">Gyges of Lydia</a> defeats the Cimmerians and sends captured city-lords to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">644 BC</span><span class="rf-text">The Cimmerians and Treres capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> except its citadel, and Gyges is killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">640 BC</span><span class="rf-text">Dugdammî falls gravely ill on campaign and takes his own life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 635 BC</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madyes">Madyes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a> defeat the Cimmerians and Treres</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Cimmerians belonged to the first wave of nomads who moved west from the region of eastern Kazakhstan or the Altai-Sayan between the 10th and the 9th to 8th centuries BC. True nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a> had emerged at the start of the first millennium BC, when climatic change turned sedentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> farmers into herders. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic-Caspian steppe</a> these migrants formed the tribal confederations of the Chernogorovka-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a> complex, which drew on the native Bilozerka culture, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a>, Aržan and Karasuk cultures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and on the Kuban culture of Ciscaucasia.</p><p>The Cimmerians lived in the Caspian and Ciscaucasian steppes, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a>, that is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, which divided them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, west to the Bosporus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch-strait">Kerch Strait</a>. The earlier claim that they lived in the Pontic Steppe lacks evidence; that region was held by the Agathyrsi. The origin of their name is uncertain: János Harmatta proposed Old Iranic *Gayamira, 'union of clans', while Askold Ivantchik derives it from *Gimĕr- or *Gimĭr-, reaching Greek through Lydian. Assyrian texts call them Gimirrāya.</p><p>They were an Iranic people who shared a language, origin and culture with the Scythians, though they may have been a mixed confederation under an Iranic aristocracy. A Thracian origin is considered untenable, arising from a confusion with their allies the Treres. Genetic studies of 2018 and 2019 examined remains of six Cimmerians, three of them buried between around 1000 and 800 BC.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Cimmerians lived an equestrian nomadic pastoralist life like that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>. West Asian sources mention their arrows, bows and horse equipment, and they used the same horse harness and weapons as the Scythians and fought on horseback. The "mare-milkers" and "milk consumers" of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> might have referred to them. Their mounted warfare was so effective that West Asian states adopted Scythian-type socketed arrowheads and sigmoid bows in the 7th and 6th centuries BC, and Assyrian sources speak of "Cimmerian bows" and "Cimmerian leather straps".</p><p>Like the Scythians, they were organised as a tribal nomadic state with its own boundaries. A supreme king ruled, his power passing within a single dynasty; three kings are known by name, Teušpâ, Dugdammî and Sandakšatru. The king could convene military assemblies of the troops, and warlords could rebel against him. The confederation may have been split into larger divisions as politics changed, much as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and the Oguz Turks were divided into wings.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> the western group became sedentary. By c. 670 BC they ruled native settlements and had founded their own, some fortified, with Ḫarzallē as Dugdammî's capital. Each settlement had a ruler whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> called a bēl ālāni, a 'city-lord', and these were both Cimmerians and members of other peoples of the kingdom. The Anatolian Cimmerians also took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> warfare and unmounted infantry. A later tradition held that they lived for a long time on the wheat stored in the underground granaries of the Phrygian village of Syassos.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Very little is known of Cimmerian religion. They left no written records, and the Assyrian and Greek texts that mention them are concerned with war and diplomacy. The burial customs of the steppe complex to which they belonged show a strong impact of the native Bilozerka culture, and remains typical of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> steppe nomads in Caucasia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a>, including kurgans with weapons, horse harness and horses, might have belonged to them. Deer stone-like carved stelae are among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asian</a> elements of the Chernogorovka-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a> complex.</p><p>The western Cimmerians who settled in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> appear to have adopted the worship of the Anatolian deity Šanta from the people of Ḫilakku and Tabal. The name of the god might appear as an element in the name of the king Sandakšatru. While they served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> of Urartu under Argišti II, some Cimmerians may have made the human funerary statues in the region of Muṣaṣir that resemble those of steppe nomads.</p><p>To their enemies the Cimmerians were outsiders who did not fear the Mesopotamian gods; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a>'s adviser Bēl-ušēzib warned the king to spy on them. Assyrian kings sought through divination to learn whether the Cimmerians would invade again. In 652 BC an oracle told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> that the goddess Ishtar had promised their defeat, and after the death of Sandakšatru Ashurbanipal thanked the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> for the fate that had struck him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Cimmerians used the same Animal Style art as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>. This art, together with dagger and arrowhead types and bits with stirrup-shaped terminals, came into the Chernogorovka-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a> complex from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a>, Aržan and Karasuk cultures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, while mace heads and bimetallic daggers followed models of the Kuban culture of Ciscaucasia. Pottery styles show the influence of the native Bilozerka culture.</p><p>The Cimmerians were part of the Scytho-Siberian horizon, and their material culture was so close to that of the early Scythians that the Chernogorovka-Novocherkassk and Proto-Scythian cultures cannot be told apart. By the time they entered West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, both had developed into the Early Scythian culture. "Scythian" arrowheads have been found among the weapons of besieging armies at ruined cities in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> where Cimmerians, but not Scythians, are attested.</p><p>Their presence in Anatolia has still largely not been identified in the archaeology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. A burial at İmirler in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a> Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> holds Early Scythian weapons and horse harness, and another, about 100 km to the east of it, contained 250 Scythian-type arrowheads. Scythian-type animal style ornaments found at Büklükale might mark a Cimmerian settlement, though the identification is uncertain. The complex also left a lasting mark on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, which adopted trousers from the steppe nomads.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Cimmerians entered West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> through the Alagir, Darial and Klukhor passes and first settled in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a>, near modern Gori, which Mesopotamian sources called māt Gamir, 'the Land of the Cimmerians'. The rivalries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, Urartu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> and smaller states offered rich opportunities, and the Cimmerians served whoever paid them best. In 714 BC they routed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusa-i">Rusa I</a> of Urartu, killing the governor of Uišini and capturing its commander in chief, which left Urartu open to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>'s campaign that year. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> they split into a western group under Teušpâ in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and an eastern group, the Indaraeans, near Mannai.</p><p>The eastern Cimmerians allied with the Mannaean king Aḫšēri, deceived Esarhaddon after promising him neutrality, and sided with the Median leader Kaštaritu when he founded the first independent kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>. In the west, Dugdammî destroyed Phrygia around c. 675 BC, after which its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a> killed himself, and by the early 660s BC the Cimmerians were masters of Anatolia. They destroyed the Greek colony of Sinope and killed its founder Habrōn, the first encounter between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and steppe nomads. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> under Gyges fought them off around c. 665–660 BC, but in 644 BC the Cimmerians and the Thracian Treres took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, killed Gyges and went on to destroy Magnesia on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Meander</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Artemision</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>.</p><p>Their power faded after Dugdammî's death in 640 BC. Around c. 635 BC the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madyes">Madyes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a> defeated them for good.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After their final defeat the Cimmerians disappeared from history and were soon assimilated by the peoples and states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, Media and Pteria. Their inroads, with those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, had upset the balance between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, Urartu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, brought several of these states down and opened the way for the empires of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a>. They also made trade routes such as the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> Road dangerous and spread steppe military technology across West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>; the Lydians themselves took up mounted cavalry.</p><p>Their memory outlived them. Late Babylonian scribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> used the name Gimirri for all the steppe nomads, including the Saka, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> names them Gōmer, closely linked to ʾAškənāz, the Scythians. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greeks</a> long feared them: an inscription of 283 BC records <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and Priene still disputing the territory of Batinētis abandoned during the invasion. In medieval Armenian tradition Cappadocia was called Gamirkʻ.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> placed the Cimmerians in a sunless land at the entrance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hades">Hades</a>, a poetic image that later writers moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the far north or Hyperborea, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/posidonius">Posidonius</a> equated them with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s story of a royal tribe that fought itself to death is now read as a folk tale, and research in the late 20th century concluded that the "Cimmerian" place names of the Pontic Steppe were invented in the 6th century BC. The racialist and antisemitic British Israelist movement built a pseudohistory on them, and in fiction Robert E. Howard made Conan the Barbarian a Cimmerian.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Cimmerians were Iranic horse nomads of the Caspian steppe, close kin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, who crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in the 8th century BC. As raiders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and conquerors they defeated Urartu, destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, until the Scythians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> defeated them around 635 BC; they then vanished into the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, leaving a long afterlife in legend.</em></p>

